sections.body:
You've already seen the core Readable stream pattern in the core-modules concept's readBody helper: attach a 'data' listener (fired once per chunk, each a Buffer by default) and an 'end' listener (fired once, after the last chunk, signaling there's nothing more to read):

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');

const readable = fs.createReadStream('./large-file.txt');
let totalBytes = 0;

readable.on('data', (chunk) => {
  totalBytes += chunk.length;   // process this chunk without holding all of them
});

readable.on('end', () => {
  console.log(`Finished reading ${totalBytes} bytes.`);
});

readable.on('error', (err) => {
  console.error('Read failed:', err.message);
});
```

Attaching a 'data' listener switches the stream into **flowing mode**, where chunks are pushed to you as events as fast as they're available. There's also a **paused mode**, where you call readable.read() to pull a chunk when you're ready, which is useful when you want to control the pace of consumption instead of reacting to a firehose of events. Everyday code either uses flowing mode directly (as above) or, more commonly, never touches these events because .pipe() (covered next) manages them. A third option, in modern code, is to consume a Readable with for await (const chunk of readable) inside an async function: the loop pulls one chunk at a time, so it respects the stream's pace, and an error on the stream is thrown into your try/catch.

The 'error' listener above isn't optional in any serious code: if a Readable stream emits an 'error' event and nothing is listening, Node treats it as an uncaught exception (covered in the error-handling module), so a stream with no error handler can crash your whole process when something goes wrong (a missing file, a permissions error, a network drop).

A Writable stream exposes .write(chunk) to send data into it and .end() to signal you're done writing, like the res.end() you've used on an HTTP response, which is itself a Writable stream:

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');

const writable = fs.createWriteStream('./output.txt');
writable.write('First line\n');
writable.write('Second line\n');
writable.end('Final line\n');   // writes one more chunk, then closes

writable.on('finish', () => console.log('All data flushed to disk.'));
writable.on('error', (err) => console.error('Write failed:', err.message));
```

The detail that matters most, and sets up the next concept, is writable.write()'s **return value**. It returns true if the stream's internal buffer is under its configured threshold (its 'highWaterMark') and you can keep writing, or **false** if the buffer has reached or passed that threshold, a signal to stop writing and wait for the stream to catch up. Ignoring the false and calling .write() anyway doesn't raise an error, but the stream's internal buffer keeps growing without bound, which is the mechanism behind the memory problems the next concept covers under the name 'backpressure.'

Wiring a Readable stream's 'data' events to a Writable stream's .write() calls by hand, while respecting write()'s return value and waiting for 'drain' when needed, is repetitive, easy-to-get-wrong bookkeeping that .pipe() automates:

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');

// Instead of reading chunks and writing them by hand, connect the two streams
fs.createReadStream('./large-file.txt')
  .pipe(fs.createWriteStream('./copy-of-large-file.txt'));
```

source.pipe(destination) does four things: it forwards every chunk read from source into destination.write(), pauses source whenever destination.write() returns false (respecting backpressure without you writing that logic), resumes source once destination emits 'drain', and calls destination.end() when source ends. It's also the pattern for streaming a file into an HTTP response without loading the whole file into memory:

```javascript
const http = require('http');
const fs = require('fs');

http.createServer((req, res) => {
  fs.createReadStream('./large-video.mp4').pipe(res);
}).listen(3000);
```

Streams can also be chained through a Transform stream in the middle, for instance compressing a file on the fly while copying it, without holding either the uncompressed *or* the compressed version entirely in memory:

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const zlib = require('zlib');

fs.createReadStream('./large-file.txt')
  .pipe(zlib.createGzip())
  .pipe(fs.createWriteStream('./large-file.txt.gz'));
```

.pipe() has a sharp edge that bites in production: **it does not forward error events between the streams it connects.** If the source in a .pipe() chain emits an error (say, the input file is deleted mid-read), that error doesn't propagate to the destination or anywhere else. You need an .on('error', ...) listener on *each* stream in the chain, or the error becomes an unhandled 'error' event on whichever stream emitted it, which can crash the process. (Nor does .pipe() close the destination when the source errors, so the destination can be left open, leaking a file descriptor.) Chained pipes without error handlers on every link are a common, easy-to-miss source of production crashes that don't show up in local testing where nothing fails mid-stream.

Node's stream.pipeline() fixes this gap. It takes the streams you'd chain with .pipe() and wires them together the same way, but funnels errors from *any* stream in the chain into a single callback (or, using the Promise-returning variant from stream/promises, a single .catch()), and destroys every stream in the chain if any one fails, which plain .pipe() doesn't reliably do:

```javascript
const { pipeline } = require('stream/promises');
const fs = require('fs');
const zlib = require('zlib');

async function compressFile(input, output) {
  await pipeline(
    fs.createReadStream(input),
    zlib.createGzip(),
    fs.createWriteStream(output)
  );
  console.log('Compression finished successfully.');
}

compressFile('./large-file.txt', './large-file.txt.gz').catch((err) => {
  console.error('Pipeline failed:', err.message);
});
```

For any pipe chain with more than one stage, or any running in production code rather than a throwaway script, pipeline() is the safer default over raw .pipe() chains.

examples.code:
const fs = require('fs');

// Loads the ENTIRE file into memory before you can do anything with it
fs.readFile('./huge-log.txt', (err, data) => {
  if (err) throw err;
  console.log(`Loaded all ${data.length} bytes at once.`);
});

// Processes the file incrementally: memory usage stays roughly constant
// regardless of the file's total size
let lineCount = 0;
let leftover = '';
fs.createReadStream('./huge-log.txt', { encoding: 'utf8' })
  .on('data', (chunk) => {
    const combined = leftover + chunk;
    const lines = combined.split('\n');
    leftover = lines.pop();   // last piece might be a partial line -- keep it for next chunk
    lineCount += lines.length;
  })
  .on('end', () => {
    if (leftover.length > 0) lineCount += 1;
    console.log(`Counted ${lineCount} lines without ever holding the whole file in memory.`);
  });

const http = require('http');
const fs = require('fs');

http.createServer((req, res) => {
  if (req.url !== '/video') {
    res.statusCode = 404;
    return res.end('Not found');
  }

  const stream = fs.createReadStream('./demo.mp4');
  stream.on('error', (err) => {
    console.error('File stream error:', err.message);
    res.statusCode = 500;
    res.end('Server error');
  });
  stream.pipe(res);
}).listen(3000);

const { pipeline } = require('stream/promises');
const fs = require('fs');
const zlib = require('zlib');

async function decompressLogFile(gzPath, outPath) {
  try {
    await pipeline(
      fs.createReadStream(gzPath),
      zlib.createGunzip(),
      fs.createWriteStream(outPath)
    );
    console.log(`Decompressed ${gzPath} -> ${outPath}`);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Decompression pipeline failed:', err.message);
  }
}

decompressLogFile('./access.log.gz', './access.log');

